# The system belongs to the owner, the repository belongs to the work

The owner's instruction, recorded 2026-10-01 after a Gradle build filled his machine's temp directory
and a session took 30 seconds to five minutes to become usable: **a factory process may make as much
mess as it likes inside this project, and must not write into the system outside it at all.** He said
he will not be watching, and that cleaning up inside the project is not required. The prohibition
outside it is absolute.

This is a different failure from the ones the rest of `harness/lessons.md` records. Those cost the
factory time. This one cost the owner's machine, which is not the factory's to spend.

## The rule

- **Every temporary file a factory-spawned process writes goes to `.kiln/scratch/` in this
  repository.** It is gitignored with all of `.kiln/`, it is disposable, and deleting it at any
  moment — including mid-build — is always safe. No permission is needed to use it, and none is
  needed to leave it dirty.
- **Never write to the system temp directory** — `%TEMP%` / `%TMP%` on Windows, `$TMPDIR` elsewhere —
  and never to any other path outside the repository. Not for a build, not for a download, not for a
  probe, not for a screenshot, not for a log.
- **`scripts/lib/scratch.mjs` and `scripts/lib/scratch.py` are the only modules that decide where
  temporary files go.** The JavaScript module exports `SCRATCH_DIR`, `scratchDirFor(name)` and
  `scratchEnv(env, name)`; the Python module exports `SCRATCH_DIR` and `scratch_dir(prefix)`. A JS
  spawner passes its environment through `scratchEnv`; a Python call passes `dir=SCRATCH_DIR` to
  `tempfile.mkdtemp()` or `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory()`. Neither language re-derives the repository
  root, and neither sets `TEMP`/`TMP`/`TMPDIR` by itself. This is the "one source of truth, imported —
  never restated" rule of `.cursor/rules/verification-discipline.mdc`, applied to the filesystem.
- **A new spawner adopts it before it ships.** `scripts/lib/gradle.mjs` is the route every Gradle
  build already takes, so wiring it there covers every build at once. A future tool that shells out
  to something heavy (a compiler, a packer, a device tool) routes through `scratchEnv` or
  `dir=SCRATCH_DIR` for the same reason.
- **A build artifact is not scratch.** `build/`, `dist/` and `captures/` stay where they are, inside
  the slot, because those are the pipeline's own outputs and its gates read them. This rule governs
  *temporary* files — the compiler daemon sessions, worker jars and working sets nobody reads back.

## What is deliberately not redirected, and why

- **`GRADLE_USER_HOME`.** It is the shared dependency cache — measured on this machine at
  `E:\Programm\Android\.gradle` — not scratch. Moving it per repository would re-download every
  dependency for every slot, which is the exact cost `.cursor/rules/build-speed.mdc` exists to
  prevent. A cache is not a mess: it is bounded, shared on purpose, and read again by the next build.
- **`build/` and `dist/`.** They are inside the repository already, which is where the rule wants
  output to be; `.gitignore` covers them and each slot's pipeline reads them.

## The one honest limit

**A reused Gradle daemon keeps the environment it was first started with.** A daemon that began
before this rule existed continues writing to the old location until it is stopped, and a build that
reuses it cannot redirect it. `--no-daemon` is *not* an acceptable fix: it is the forbidden flag that
costs the measured 6.3 s → 2.3 s regression `build-speed.mdc` records. The remedy is to stop a stale
daemon once (`Stop-Process` on the Gradle JVM, or the wrapper's own `--stop`), not to fork a fresh one
per build. This limit is written down rather than papered over, because a rule that claims more than
it delivers is the failure mode `verification-discipline.mdc` names.

## How it is verified

`build.scratch` is a check in `scripts/core/build.mjs`, run by `node scripts/verify.mjs` in the
`invariants` group. It reads the repository and fails when:

- `.kiln/scratch/` does not exist, is not inside the repository, or is not gitignored;
- `scripts/lib/scratch.mjs` stops exporting `scratchEnv`, or stops setting `TEMP`, `TMP` and `TMPDIR`
  together, or derives its root from anywhere but its own file location;
- `scripts/lib/scratch.py` is missing or stops defining `SCRATCH_DIR`;
- `scripts/lib/gradle.mjs` stops routing its spawn environment through `scratchEnv` — the wiring that
  makes the rule true rather than asserted;
- any module under `scripts/` names `os.tmpdir()` / `tmpdir()` as a write target, which is how the
  system temp directory gets chosen by accident;
- any Python file under `scripts/` calls `tempfile.mkdtemp()`, `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory()` or
  `tempfile.gettempdir()` without `dir=SCRATCH_DIR`, reported as a `file:line`.

Its inline fixtures drive each of those with a synthetic tree whose verdict is known, because the
lesson this repository keeps re-learning is that **a rule only a document asserts is a rule nobody
follows** (`AGENTS.md`, self-learning loop). The prose above is what a builder reads; the check is
what makes it so.

The measured state this rule was written into, so the size of the change is auditable rather than
implied: **14 JavaScript fixture sandboxes** (four of them running on every `verify.mjs` — the
`manifest`, `uiux`, `settings` and `localization` checks) and **11 Python `tempfile` calls across six
scripts** used the system temp directory, and all 25 now use `SCRATCH_DIR`. The rule caught those by
being written as a sweep of the whole `scripts/` tree rather than as a note about the one file that
had been observed misbehaving.

The rule does not, and cannot, observe a process it did not spawn. A tool the agent runs by hand, a
provider SDK's own cache, or a browser's download directory is outside any sensor here — the
instruction to keep them inside `.kiln/scratch/` is the agent's obligation, not the check's finding.
